== 5. Of the Choice of Persons Fit for Marriage

To make a contract, two things are required: first the choice, and then the consent of the parties.

Choice is an inquiry after persons who are marriageable.

Persons marriageable are those who are fit and able for the married state.

This fitness or ability is known and discerned by certain signs, which are either essential to the contract or accidental.

An essential sign is one without which the contract in hand becomes an utter nullity. And of this sort there are principally five.

The first is the distinction of the sex, which is either male or female. The male is man, of a superior sex, fit for procreation. The female is woman, of an inferior sex, fit to conceive and bear children. 1 Cor 11:7: “A man ought not to cover his head, since he is the image and glory of God; but the woman is the glory of man.” 1 Tim 2:12: “I do not permit a woman to teach or to exercise authority over a man; she is to remain quiet.” By this distinction is condemned that unnatural and monstrous sin of uncleanness between parties of the same sex,#footnote[Rom 1:26] commonly called sodomy; and also the mixing of the#footnote[Species.] #emph[kinds] of creatures, when one kind commits filthiness and abomination with another.#footnote[Lev 18:22, 23.]

The second sign is the just and lawful distance of blood.

Distance of blood is just and lawful when neither of the persons who are to be married comes near to the kindred of their flesh, or to the flesh of their flesh. For so the Scripture speaks, Lev 18:6: “None of you#super[+] shall come near to any of the flesh of his flesh,” or to “the kindred of his flesh.”#footnote[Ebr. שְׁאֵר בְּשָׂרוֹ] Here it is to be observed that by #emph[a man’s flesh] is meant that substance which is of himself, or of which he himself consists. And by the #emph[flesh of his flesh] is meant that which next and immediately issues out of the flesh of which he consists. From this it follows that the touching or coming near of flesh to flesh is not #emph[spoken of strangers,] but #emph[of those] only who are of kindred.

Kindred is of two sorts: kindred by blood (consanguinity), or kindred by marriage (affinity).

Kindred in consanguinity are those who issue from one and the same common blood or stock. And there are certain degrees by which kinsmen of this sort are distinguished from one another.

A degree is a generation or descent between two persons who are of the same blood. For one person alone cannot make a degree, unless he is considered in relation to another, in regard of generation.

Now many degrees continued or derived from one and the same head make a line. And a line is twofold: the direct line, or the crosswise line, commonly called the collateral.

In the direct line, count how many persons there are, and there are so many degrees, leaving out only the person who is in question in the case at hand, and from whom we begin the count.

#[
- Abraham.
- Isaac.
- Jacob.
- Joseph.
] <diagram>

Here #emph[Joseph] is three degrees distant from #emph[Abraham,] #emph[Jacob] two, #emph[Isaac] one, or the first.

The direct line is either ascending or descending.

The direct line ascending is the lineage of all the ancestors, and it has several degrees. Of the first degree are the father and the mother. Of the second, the grandfather on both the father’s and the mother’s side, that is, the father of my father and the father of my mother; and the grandmother, that is, my father’s mother and my mother’s mother. Of the third, the great-grandfather, or the father of my grandfather; and the great-grandmother, or the mother of my grandfather. Of the fourth, the great-grandfather’s father, and the great-grandfather’s or great-grandmother’s mother. Of the fifth, the great-grandfather’s grandfather, and the great-grandfather’s or great-grandmother’s grandmother. Of the sixth, the great-great-grandfather’s grandfather, and the great-great-grandfather’s or great-great-grandmother’s grandmother.

The direct line descending is the lineage of a man’s own posterity. And it also has several degrees. The first is the son and the daughter; the second, the grandson and the granddaughter; the third, the grandson’s son and the granddaughter’s daughter. The fourth is the grandson’s grandson, or the son in the fourth degree of lineal descent, and the granddaughter’s granddaughter, or the daughter in the same degree of descent. The fifth is he and she who are three lineal descents from the child’s child. And so on with the rest in their order, by whatever names they are called and known.

Now in this direct line, whether ascending or descending, the person about whom the case or question is raised comes near to the kindred of his flesh. And therefore marriages in this line are prohibited without end, without any limit; so that if #emph[Adam] himself were alive now, he could not marry by the law of God, because he would come near to the kindred of his own flesh, as #emph[Moses] speaks. The reason for this is that in the direct line, superiors and inferiors are to each other as parents and children. And since the kinship between parents and children is the first and nearest of all, their union in marriage must be most unclean, and contrary to nature.

The collateral or crooked line is the one that runs alongside the direct line, whether ascending or descending, in the ranks of the kindred. And it is either unequal or equal.

The unequal is the line in which the person in question and his kinsman are unequally distant from the common stock, or head of the kindred.

And in this line, as many degrees as the kinsman who is furthest off is distant from the common stock, so many degrees are he and the kinsman in question distant from each other.

#strong[For example,]

#[
- Terah.
  - Abraham—
  - Nahor
    - Bethuel
      - Laban.
] <diagram>

Here #emph[Laban,] being removed furthest off, that is, three degrees from the common stock #emph[Terah,] is likewise three degrees distant from #emph[Abraham,] his great-uncle, and #emph[Abraham] as many from him.

Furthermore, kinsmen in this line are of two sorts: some are superiors to the person in question, and some are inferiors to him.

Superiors are those who are akin to his father and mother, #emph[on one side or the other;] and that either nearer or further off.

Those who are #emph[closely] akin to them are these.

#set enum(numbering: "I.")

+ The uncle on the father’s side, or the father’s brother; the aunt on the father’s side, or the father’s sister. The uncle on the mother’s side, or the mother’s brother; the aunt on the mother’s side, or the mother’s sister.
+ The great-uncle, or the grandfather’s brother; the great-aunt, or the grandfather’s sister. The great-uncle, or the grandmother’s brother, and the great-aunt, or the grandmother’s sister.
+ The great-uncle on the father’s side, or the great-grandfather’s brother; and the great-aunt on the father’s side, or the great-grandfather’s sister. The great-uncle on the mother’s side, or the great-grandmother’s brother; and the great-aunt on the mother’s side, or the great-grandmother’s sister.
+ The great-grand-uncle on the father’s side, or the great-grandfather’s father’s brother; and the great-grand-aunt on the same side, or the great-grandfather’s father’s sister. The great-grand-uncle on the mother’s side, or the great-grandmother’s mother’s brother; and the great-grand-aunt on the same side, or the great-grandmother’s mother’s sister.

Now these, in regard of their inferiors, stand in the place of parents, and therefore they come near to the kindred of their flesh.

The superiors who are #emph[distantly] akin to the parents on one side are these.

+ The son and daughter of the great-uncle or great-aunt on the father’s side, or of the great-uncle or great-aunt on the mother’s side.
+ The grandson and granddaughter of the great-uncle or great-aunt on the father’s side, or of the great-uncle or great-aunt on the mother’s side,#footnote[Sobriuo propior.] #emph[near to the first cousin.]

And these, as they stand compared with their inferiors, do not come near to the kindred of their flesh.

The equal collateral line is the one in which kinsmen are equally distant from their head.

Kinsmen of this line are:

+ Whole brothers, that is, brothers by the same father and mother; or half brothers, that is, brothers by the same father but not by the same mother. Again, whole sisters by the same father and mother, or half sisters by one of them and not by both.
+ The children of brothers, or first cousins: that is, the uncle’s sons or daughters, or the aunt’s sons or daughters. The children of sisters, or first cousins: that is, the aunt’s sons or daughters, who are the children of two sisters.
+ The cousin who is the son of the great-uncle on the father’s or mother’s side, and the cousin who is the son of the great-aunt on the father’s or mother’s side. The cousin who is the daughter of the great-uncle on the father’s or mother’s side, and the cousin who is the daughter of the great-aunt on the same sides.

In this line, as many degrees as the party in question is removed from the common stock, twice as many degrees is he removed from his kinsman in question. Take the case between #emph[Bethuel] and #emph[Isaac,] as in this example.

#[
- Terah
  - Abraham—
    - Isaac.
  - Nahor
    - Bethuel.
] <diagram>

Here #emph[Bethuel,] being two degrees removed from #emph[Terah,] is four degrees distant from #emph[Isaac.]

Now he who is in the second degree of this line comes near to the kindred of his flesh; and for that reason the marriages of brothers and sisters are unlawful and forbidden.

But several things are alleged against this.

#emph[Objection 1.] The sons of #emph[Adam,] who were the first brothers in the world, married their sisters. Marriages in this degree may therefore seem to be lawful. #emph[Answer.] The case of those persons in those times was a case of necessity, which could not be avoided, and so it was warranted by divine dispensation. For God had given them his own express commandment in this matter: “Be fruitful and multiply” (Gen 1:28). Besides this, there were then no other women in the world by whom they might have offspring and posterity, but only their sisters. From this it is plain that God’s will was to permit them to take even their sisters as their wives. But now, outside this special dispensation from God, such matching is utterly unlawful, being an open breach of another express commandment given afterward by God himself (Lev 18:9, 11). To this purpose #emph[Augustine] says, in answer to the example put forward,#footnote[De ciuit. Dei. lib. 15. ca. 16.] “The practice of those first times was then backed by urgent necessity; the more ancient it was, the more damnable it proved afterward, when it was restrained by God’s commandment.”

#emph[Objection 2.] #emph[Sarah] was #emph[Abraham’s] sister, and yet #emph[Abraham] took her as his wife. Gen 20:12: “She really is my sister, the daughter of my father—though not the daughter of my mother—and she became my wife.” Gen 11:29: “Abram and Nahor took wives for themselves. Abram’s wife was named Sarai.” #emph[Answer.] On this instance there are several opinions. The Jews grant this much: that she was called by a name common to all kinswomen, namely #emph[a sister,] yet she was in fact #emph[Abraham’s] niece, the sister of #emph[Lot] and the daughter of #emph[Haran, Abraham’s] brother, whom #emph[Moses] elsewhere calls #emph[Iscah] (Gen 11:29). Others think that she was stepdaughter to #emph[Terah, Abraham’s] father, because #emph[Abraham] himself says, “She is the daughter of my father, not of my mother.” Others are of the opinion that she was #emph[Haran’s] stepdaughter, and after his death was taken into the family of #emph[Terah,] her grandfather, who also stood in the place of her father, now dead; and so she was called his daughter. Now however hard it is to find certain truth amid this variety of judgments, this is beyond doubt: that God in those days tolerated many things which nevertheless he did not altogether approve.

#emph[Objection 3.] 2 Sam 13:13: Tamar #emph[says to her brother] Amnon, “Please speak to the king, for he will not withhold me from you.” #emph[Answer.] The speech of #emph[Tamar] is not to be taken simply, as if she desired what she said, or as if what she wished him to do were lawful to be done. She only sought to put him off by giving him this advice, for her own deliverance from the danger she was in at that moment. Or at least it may be said that she wrongly supposed it was lawful for the king to grant such a request, if her brother #emph[Amnon] had made it to him. Whichever way this speech is taken, nothing such as is claimed can be gathered from it.

Now beyond the second degree of the equal collateral line, it seems that those who are joined to one another in marriage do not touch the kindred of their flesh. And therefore the law of God does not forbid first cousins, that is, the children of brothers and sisters, to marry.

#emph[Indeed, if examples carry weight here, the Scripture approves such marriages. For it is written that Jacob married Rachel, the daughter of Laban, his mother’s brother (Gen 28:2). Again, that] “Mahlah, Tirzah, Hoglah, Milcah, and Noah, the daughters of Zelophehad, were married to their father’s brothers’ sons” #emph[(Num 36:11). And] “Othniel son of Caleb’s brother Kenaz captured Kiriath-sepher, and Caleb gave his daughter Acsah to him in marriage” (Josh 15:17).

#emph[However, in this case the rule of the apostle is to be followed:] that nothing be done, even in #emph[things that are otherwise lawful,] by which a man’s brother may stumble, or be offended, or made weak #emph[(Rom 14:21). Therefore it is fitting that such marriages should not be made, because at times they may give offense in regard of the law of the magistrate, and because they come somewhat near to the bounds and limits of blood and stock which God has set down in his word.] Augustine, #emph[in the fifteenth book of] De ciuitate Dei, #emph[ch. 16, says to this purpose,] Experti autem sumus, #emph[etc. That is,] “We have found by experience how rarely custom has admitted those marriages between first cousins, because of the nearness of blood they have to the degree of brothers and sisters, though such marriages were warranted by the laws, since the law of God has not forbidden them, nor as yet the law of man. Nevertheless the practice, lawful in itself, was forborne all the more because it came near to what was unlawful; and so marriage with a first cousin seemed in a way to be marriage with a man’s own brother or sister, #emph[etc.] However, there is no question that in these times it has been well provided by laws that such marriages should not be undertaken. For not only is the multiplying of alliances hindered by them; this harm also grows from them: the friendship and love which affinity procures between men who are strangers cannot be enlarged and increased where men do not go outside the limits and bounds of their own blood. Indeed, there is naturally in man a commendable measure of natural shame, by which, considering nearness in blood, he acknowledges a due debt of modesty and honor to his nearest kindred; and so he restrains himself, in regard of them, from the occasions and acts of concupiscence to which he is inclined in respect of others. And this is why, generally, the modesty that ought to be in those who intend marriage avoids, as much as it can, these and similar unions.” #emph[The same is the judgment of] Ambrose #emph[in one of his letters to his friend] Paternus,#footnote[Epistol. li. 8. epist. 66.] #emph[in which he dissuades him from marrying his son to his first cousin, on the grounds alleged above by] Augustine. Ambrose #emph[adds this as well: that though it were lawful in itself, being nowhere forbidden by God, yet being inexpedient in several respects, it ought not to be done, according to the saying of the apostle,] “Everything is lawful for me,#footnote[1 Cor 6.] but not everything is expedient.”

But it is maintained on the contrary that God by express law forbids marriage with the aunt, the father’s sister (Lev 18:12), and with the mother’s sister (v. 13), and with the father’s brother’s wife, who is also the aunt (v. 14). Therefore, by the law of proportion, he forbids marrying the children of these persons. For in nature it is a settled case: if a man may not marry the mother, much less may he marry the daughter.

#emph[Answer.] The argument is faulty and concludes nothing to the purpose. For the reason is not the same in both. In the direct line of consanguinity, those who are sisters to the father and mother stand in the place of parents, and their nephews and nieces are to them as children. Therefore marriages among them can stand by no law, whether of God, of nature, or the positive laws of nations. It is not so with first cousins. For since they have no such relation, there is in their marriages, by the law of #emph[Moses,] no uncovering of nakedness, nor any breach of public propriety. The blessing of God on some of them mentioned in Scripture, as on #emph[Jacob] and #emph[Rachel] and others like them, plainly shows that such marriages were lawful in themselves and pleasing to him. And so much concerning kindred by blood, or consanguinity.

Kindred in affinity is that by which the bounds of two kindreds are brought into the fellowship of one and the same family; or by which persons who are not of one blood are allied to each other by marriage. That is why it is called affinity: because it joins into one the bounds of kindreds which before were apart.

Affinity, or alliance, grows by a mingling or sharing of blood, in the joining of man and wife together in lawful marriage. For a bare and simple contract does not properly make alliance between the parties; marriage does, after the contract is finished and consummated.

Again, the same alliance ceases and ends at the death of either party, betrothed or married; yet the prohibition of union in marriage remains and stands in force, for the preservation of public propriety.

Now there is a kind of likeness and proportion between kindred by blood and kindred by marriage, not only in regard of degrees and line, but also in respect of the prohibition of degrees.

Those who are of alliance are commonly brought under these heads.

+ The father-in-law, or the husband’s or wife’s father. The mother-in-law, or the husband’s or wife’s mother.
+ The mother’s husband, or stepfather to those born of another man. The father’s wife, or stepmother to those born of another woman.
+ The wife’s father’s father, or grandfather, and the wife’s mother’s mother, or grandmother; that is, the grandfather and grandmother of the wife.
+ The son-in-law, or daughter’s husband, and the daughter-in-law, or son’s wife.
+ The granddaughter’s husband, and the grandson’s wife.
+ The stepson, that is, the son of a man by another wife, or of a woman by another husband. The stepdaughter, that is, the daughter of a man by another wife, or of a woman by another husband.
+ The stepson’s son, that is, the son of him who is a man’s son by another woman, or a woman’s son by another man. And the stepdaughter’s daughter, that is, the daughter of her who is a man’s daughter by another woman, or a woman’s daughter by another husband.
+ The husband’s or wife’s brother, and the husband’s sister, or brother’s wife.
+ The sister’s husband, and the brother’s wife.

Concerning affinity, I propose these rules.

I. In whatever degree a man is of consanguinity to a wife, in the same degree of affinity he is to the wife’s husband. And the reverse. For example:

#[
- Isaac
  - Esau.
  - Jacob.
    - Joseph—
  - Joseph

- Samuel
  - Aaron.
  - Eli.
    - Maria.
  - Levi.
] <diagram>

Here, if #emph[Mary,] #emph[Joseph’s] wife, is two degrees distant from #emph[Esau,] #emph[Joseph’s] uncle on the father’s side, then #emph[Joseph] himself is also two degrees distant from #emph[Aaron,] the brother of #emph[Eli] his father-in-law. If #emph[Mary] the wife is one degree distant from her own father #emph[Eli,] and two from her grandfather #emph[Samuel,] then her husband is also one degree distant from #emph[Eli] his father-in-law, and two from #emph[Samuel] his wife’s grandfather. Another example:

#[
- Herod.
  - Herod.
  - Philip, whose wife is Herodias.
] <diagram>

Here, since #emph[Herod] is in the first degree of consanguinity with his brother #emph[Philip,] #emph[Herodias,] his brother’s wife, is in the first degree of affinity to him.

II. #emph[Rule.] Kin by marriage within the degrees, both of the direct and the collateral line, come near to the kindred of their flesh in the same way that kin by blood do in the same lines; and the like degrees in both are forbidden. Therefore in the direct line of affinity it is just as unlawful for the father-in-law to marry the daughter-in-law, or the son-in-law to marry the mother-in-law, as it is for the father to marry the daughter, or the son the mother. Again, in the collateral line, it is as unlawful for the uncle on the mother’s side to marry the wife of his sister’s son (and so in other degrees) as it is for him to marry in the same degrees of consanguinity. The reason is that in these lines, as well as in those of consanguinity, superiors and inferiors are to each other as parents and children.

#emph[From this, among other things, it may be gathered that it is in no way lawful for a man, when his wife is dead, to marry her sister.] Lev 18:16: “You must not uncover the nakedness of your brother’s wife; it is your brother’s nakedness.” #emph[And Lev 20:21:] “If a man marries his brother’s wife, it is an act of impurity. He has uncovered the nakedness of his brother.”

Against this doctrine it is objected, first, that God commands a man not to take a wife together with her sister during her life (Lev 18:18). In that place, it is said, he does not simply forbid a man to marry two sisters one after another, but to marry them both together; and therefore, after the wife is dead, a man may marry her sister.

#emph[Answer.] The place is a flat prohibition of the sin of #emph[polygamy.] For in the Hebrew idiom, to take a wife to her sister is nothing else than to take two wives, one to another. The same form of speech is used elsewhere by #emph[Moses,] as in Exod 26:3.

“Five of the curtains are to be joined together,” \[the woman to her sister\] that is, one to another; “and the other five are to be joined” \[the woman to her sister\] that is, the one to the other. Ezek 1:9: “Their wings were joined” \[the woman to her sister\] that is, the one to the other. Again, #emph[Moses] himself there gives two reasons against #emph[polygamy.] The one is that the man is to love, cherish and comfort his wife; whereas if he took to himself another besides her, he would greatly vex his first, lawful wife. The other is that by this means he would uncover the nakedness of his wife; that is, he would deal very dishonorably with her to whom he was lawfully married before.

#emph[Objection 2.] The Lord commanded by a special law that if a man’s brother died without offspring, he should take his brother’s wife and raise up offspring for him (Deut 25:5).

#emph[Answer.] The words of #emph[Moses] are a special exception to a general law set down in Lev 18:16: “You must not uncover the nakedness of your brother’s wife; it is your brother’s nakedness.” And they are directed particularly to the Israelites, for a special cause: that when the elder brother died without offspring, the name of the firstborn might not be blotted out, but the family might be preserved in that name by raising up offspring for him. Since this was a privilege belonging only to the Israelites, on that special ground, it must not be extended to any other sort of men whatever. Nor is the text to be understood only of more distant kinsmen, as some think, but also of the nearest of the kindred by blood. This appears plainly from the history of #emph[Ruth,] in these words of #emph[Naomi] to her daughters-in-law (Ruth 1:11): “Return home, my daughters. Why would you#super[+] go with me? Are there still sons in my womb to become your#super[+] husbands? Return home, my daughters. Go on, for I am too old to have another husband,” etc.

III. #emph[Rule.] The wife’s kinsman by blood is of affinity to her husband only; but to the husband’s kindred by blood that kinsman has no affinity at all. So, the other way, it is true that though the husband’s kinsman by blood is allied to his wife, yet he has no alliance to those who are her kinsmen by blood. The reason is that between the kindred by blood on the man’s side and the kindred by blood on the woman’s side there is no affinity. For the prohibition of the degrees of affinity has force only in those who are the cause of it, and goes no further. Now since the cause lies only in the married persons themselves, the effect may not reasonably be extended to those who are of the same blood with either of them.

From this rule it follows that the kindred by blood of both married persons may lawfully marry one another. The stepson may marry the daughter of his stepmother. A father, and his son by another wife, may marry a mother and her daughter. Two brothers may lawfully marry two sisters.

IV. #emph[Rule.] Kindred of affinity to the wife#footnote[Beza de repudijs. 82.] are also kindred of affinity to her husband. But how? Not strictly, but only in a certain sense, and as it were of affinity. So also the other way: kindred of alliance to the husband are, as it were, of alliance to his wife. For example, the wife of my father-in-law, who is not my wife’s own mother but her stepmother, is, as it were, my mother-in-law. For she is, as it were, of affinity to me, because she is truly of affinity to my wife.

Those who are of affinity only in a certain sense cannot fittingly marry one another. And therefore the son-in-law may not marry his wife’s stepmother, because, being of affinity to his wife, she stands in the place of a mother-in-law to him. Nor may the daughter-in-law marry her husband’s stepfather, because he is, as it were, a stepfather to her. Again, my sister’s husband may not marry my brother’s wife, for they are in a certain sense brother and sister to each other. The reason for this is that the wife is held in law to be a#footnote[Cuius nuptias inire non licet, eius nec coniugis licet [Whom one may not marry, neither may one marry that person’s spouse].] part of her husband; and therefore, if affinity hinders marriage with one of the married couple, it equally hinders marriage with the other, in the like case and respect.

And so much concerning the distance of blood, which must most necessarily be observed in choosing persons fit for marriage. For in the eighteenth chapter of #emph[Leviticus] there are sixteen different sorts of persons with whom marriage is forbidden. The first is the mother. The second is the stepmother. The third, the sister by the same father and mother. The fourth, the half sister, by one of the parents only. The fifth is the granddaughter by the son. The sixth, the granddaughter by the daughter. The seventh, the father’s sister. The eighth, the uncle’s wife, that is, the father’s brother’s wife. The ninth, the mother’s sister. The tenth, the daughter-in-law, or son’s wife. The eleventh, the brother’s wife. The twelfth, the wife’s mother. The thirteenth, the stepdaughter, that is, the wife’s daughter by another husband, or the husband’s daughter by a former wife. The fourteenth is the stepson’s daughter, that is, the daughter of him who was the wife’s son by another husband. The fifteenth, the stepdaughter’s daughter. The sixteenth is the wife’s sister. And not only these, but all others that answer to them, in the same or a like degree, are by equal proportion forbidden to be taken in marriage.

It is alleged that the prohibitions mentioned in that chapter are merely Mosaic, that is, ceremonial, and therefore do not bind us now in the New Testament.

#emph[Answer.] That is a flat untruth, as will appear by these reasons.

First, the Canaanites, long before these laws were given by #emph[Moses] to the Israelites, were grievously afflicted for breaking and despising them. This may be gathered from what is written in the same chapter of #emph[Leviticus,] #footnote[Chapter 18.] v. 3: “You#super[+] must not follow the practices of the land of Canaan … You#super[+] must not walk in their customs.” And v. 24: “Do not defile yourselves#super[+] by any of these practices, for by all these things the nations I am driving out before you#super[+] have defiled themselves.” v. 25: “Even the land has become defiled, so I am punishing it for its sin, and the land will vomit out its inhabitants.”

#emph[Secondly, the prophets themselves after Moses are accustomed to count these prohibitions not ceremonial but moral.] Ezek 22:10: “In you they have uncovered the nakedness of their fathers; in you they violate women during their menstrual impurity.” Amos 2:7: “A man and his father have relations with the same girl and so profane My holy name.”

#emph[Thirdly, the same laws are also urged in the New Testament, in which the legal ceremonies came to an end and were finished.] Mark 6:18: John #emph[said to] Herod, “It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife!” 1 Cor 5:1: “It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you#super[+], and of a kind that is intolerable even among pagans: A man has his father’s wife.”

Fourthly, nature itself, by its own instinct, abhors such unions, and the prohibitions of them have sufficient ground even in her principles. It is for this reason that, in all ages, not only the laws of the Roman emperors, but also the civil canons and constitutions of men very skilled in that field, have been expressly made against such persons as have offended in those unions forbidden by the law of #emph[Moses.]

#emph[Fifthly, the Lord himself has threatened many great punishments to be inflicted for breaking these same laws.] Lev 20:11: “If a man lies with his father’s wife, he has uncovered his father’s nakedness. Both must surely be put to death; their blood is upon them.” v. 12: “If a man lies with his daughter-in-law, both must surely be put to death. They have acted perversely; their blood is upon them.” v. 14: “If a man marries both a woman and her mother, it is depraved. Both he and they must be burned in the fire.” v. 17: “If a man marries his sister, whether the daughter of his father or of his mother, and they have sexual relations, it is a disgrace. They must be cut off in the sight of their people.” v. 20: “If a man lies with his uncle’s wife, he has uncovered the nakedness of his uncle. They will bear their sin; they shall die childless.” Deut 23:2: “No one of illegitimate birth may enter the assembly of the #smallcaps[Lord], nor may any of his descendants, even to the tenth generation.” Deut 27:20: “Cursed is he who sleeps with his father’s wife.”

The third essential sign of a person marriageable is ability and fitness for procreation. And this, in a holy and modest way, is always presumed to be in the party contracted, unless the contrary is plainly known and discerned by some evident infirmity in the body.

From this I gather that it is unlawful to make a contract with a person who is unfit for the use of marriage, either by natural constitution of body or by accident: for example, by reason of sickness, or of frigidity, or of incurable palsy, or lastly of the loss of the parts belonging to generation. These and similar impediments have force, even if a contract has already been made, to make it an utter nullity. For God makes known his will in them: that he does not approve of such a betrothal, but would have it dissolved.

Again, a contract made between two persons who are#footnote[Impuberes.] under age is to be held and counted unlawful. And even if it is made by the consent or command of parents, it is of no weight. This always holds, unless it is ratified by a new consent of the parties after they come of age, or unless they have in the meantime had private and carnal intercourse with one another.

Again, take the case that two are betrothed, of whom one is under age and the other of full age. The party of full age ought to wait and look for the time when the outcome of the betrothal already made may be seen. And the one under age may not break off consent, either before or when he or she comes of age, but must wait for the fulfillment of the contract when he or she is out of minority.

The fourth essential sign is a sound and healthy constitution of body, free from incurably contagious diseases.

From this it follows that a promise of marriage made between two persons, one of whom has a disease so loathsome that the other, on good ground, cannot possibly endure familiar fellowship and company with him, is utterly unlawful, even though the disease does not wholly hinder the use of the body.

Of this sort are leprosy, syphilis, and the like. For there cannot be any marital use between those who are tainted with such contagious diseases without evident danger of infecting each other, and those also who keep company with them, and others. Indeed, by this means the offspring of their bodies, growing from a corrupted seed, are born to lifelong misery, and to the great hurt and hindrance of the commonwealth. So the case is plain: such marriages cannot be undertaken with a good conscience.

Now if one party has a disease which, though not contagious, is either incurable or so#footnote[Turpi.] loathsome that the other has just cause to loathe his company (for example, if he is sick of a lethargy, of epilepsy, or similar diseases), then it is lawful to marry him, because such a contract is not expressly forbidden, nor is it plainly hurtful to the commonwealth. Yet it is not expedient. For since the disease is loathsome to the sound party, the marriage also will become unprofitable, and that party will be in danger of breaking the commandment of the Moral law in which God forbids adultery.

The fifth essential sign of a person marriageable is freedom from marriage, by which both parties who enter the contract are so at liberty that neither the man has another wife at present, or is promised to another, nor the woman has another husband, or is promised to another. The reason is that God himself counts that marriage alone lawful in which one man is joined to one woman, and they both into one flesh. And so he judges it unlawful for one man to have more wives than one, and equally for one woman to have more husbands than one at the same time.

From this it follows that it is against the law of God, and the first institution of marriage, for a contract to be made between persons of whom one is already betrothed to another. For as long as the first promise stands in force, the man can no more be betrothed to two women than he may be the husband of two wives. For the betrothed woman, by the bond of her promise to the man, becomes a wife; and the man, by the bond of his promise to the woman, becomes a husband (Deut 22:23, 24).

Again, there can be no betrothal, and so no marriage, with one who either has been divorced without sufficient cause, or has wrongfully forsaken and rejected a lawful mate before. To this purpose our Savior Christ says in Matt 19:9: “I tell you#super[+] that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman, commits adultery; and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery.” And Saint #emph[Paul,] 1 Cor 7:11: “But if she departs, she must remain unmarried or else be reconciled to her husband. And a husband must not divorce his wife.”

So far I have treated the essential marks, which belong to the being of marriage. Now I come to those that are accidental.

An accidental mark of a person fit for marriage is one which belongs not to the being of marriage but to its well-being, that is, to the holiness and purity of that state. And of this sort there are three principal ones.

The first is parity or equality in regard of the Christian religion. For in marriage special care must be taken that believers are matched with believers, and Christians with Christians; not believers with infidels, or Christians with pagans. For the first kind of marriage alone, and always, is to be made and kept; the second is to be avoided, and indeed prohibited by positive laws and constitutions, the breach of which in such cases is to be severely punished. The neglect of this point was one principal cause of the destruction of the old world by the flood. Gen 6:2: “The sons of God saw that the daughters of men were beautiful, and they took as wives whomever they chose.” #emph[Abraham] observed it in the marriage of his son #emph[Isaac,] and for that purpose took an oath of his servant. Gen 24:3: “I will have you swear by the #smallcaps[Lord], the God of heaven and the God of earth, that you will not take a wife for my son from the daughters of the Canaanites among whom I am dwelling.” And following that holy example, #emph[Isaac] also gives a charge to his son #emph[Jacob:] “Do not take a wife from the Canaanite women” (Gen 28:1). Afterward an express law of God was given by #emph[Moses] concerning the Gentiles who lived in that land. Exod 34:15: “Do not make a covenant with the inhabitants of the land,” etc. Verse 16: “And when you take some of their daughters as brides for your sons, their daughters will prostitute themselves to their gods and cause your sons to do the same.” And the same law was urged by #emph[Ezra] the priest in his time. Ezra 10:11: “Separate yourselves#super[+] from the people of the land and from your#super[+] foreign wives.” Lastly, Saint #emph[Paul] exhorts the Corinthians, 2 Cor 6:14: “Do not be unequally yoked with unbelievers.” And Titus 3:10: “Reject a heretic after a first and second admonition.”

But suppose it happens that the betrothal is already made, and that both parties were believers at first, or only one of them; or that at first both were unbelievers, and afterward one of them is converted to the faith; or further, suppose that both were believers at the contract, yet some time after, one of them becomes an infidel, that is, a Jew, a Turk, or an obstinate heretic who denies the faith and razes even the very foundation of religion. What is to be done in this case? Surely even such a betrothal, once made and completed, if there are no other impediments but these, is to stand and continue firm and unchangeable, as long as the unbelieving party willingly yields and submits himself or herself to the performance of the marriage duties that properly belong to man and wife. My reason is the saying of #emph[Paul:] “If a brother has an unbelieving wife and she is willing to live with him, he must not divorce her.” Again: “And if a woman has an unbelieving husband and he is willing to live with her, she must not divorce him” (1 Cor 7:12, 13).

It may be alleged here that the sin of adultery dissolves the bond both of contract and of marriage, and therefore idolatry or unbelief does so much more, being a sin far more detestable than adultery. I answer that the question is not which of these is the greater sin, or more heinous in the sight of God (for in both respects unbelief far exceeds the other), but which of them is more contrary to the nature and condition of marriage. Now the sin of adultery is the only one that breaks the bond and renounces the faith pledged in marriage, and is the true cause of a divorce; idolatry or unbelief is not.

The second note is parity or equality in regard of age and condition.

First, of age. For though the marriage of persons whose years are far unequal is not expressly forbidden in the word, yet it agrees with the rules of expediency and decency that the aged should marry the aged, and the younger the younger.

The reasons are these. First, because by this means the comforts of this fellowship will likely always be equal, and so bring the more contentment to either party. Secondly, these unequal marriages often give offense to others. Thirdly, in some cases they cannot but prove troublesome even to the parties themselves. For when a man of great years marries a very young woman, or a young stripling marries an aged woman, the elder party, growing weak and impotent, may the sooner become unfit for marriage duties; and the younger, being of greater strength and ability, is the more in danger of being exposed to sexual incontinence, and that in the highest degree, unless restrained by God’s special grace. For this reason it was a great fault with which one #emph[Apuleius] was justly charged: that while he was only a very young man, he was married to a wife sixty years of age.

Secondly, of condition and standing. For this also answers to the apostle’s rule, who exhorts men to think on and to do those things which are “true … right … admirable … of good report” (Phil 4:8). So it is a seemly and commendable practice that the prince, the nobleman, the freeman, the gentleman, the yeoman, and so on, should be joined in fellowship with those who are of the same or a like condition with themselves, and not otherwise. For this reason the ancient Romans are greatly to be commended, who (as we read in their histories) had such careful regard to their marriages in this respect that they made laws and constitutions to restrain inequality of worldly standing in such unions, by this means preventing much confusion and disturbance in families and in other orders of men. With this agrees the saying of #emph[Tertullian,] #footnote[Tertul. ad vxor. li. 2.] “If a free woman had betrothed herself to a man who was another’s slave, and, after being warned of it three times by the one who had authority over her, still persisted in her purpose, she should by the law lose her freedom.”

The third note is public propriety and good name, by which the contract made becomes a matter of good report, well thought of and spoken of widely. “Whatever is of good report … think on these things” (Phil 4:8). From this I gather:

I. It is an unseemly thing for a man to make a promise of marriage to a woman who has been deflowered before, or who has been, or is, or may be convicted of adultery and uncleanness. Indeed, I add further that a contract made with one whom the man himself has deflowered before is unlawful by the law of God. For by divine law the adulterer and the adulteress should be put to death and cut off from human society (Lev 20:10).

II. It is altogether inexpedient for a woman to be married to a man who has a concubine, unless he first renounces her and testifies to it by true and unfeigned repentance. The reason is plain. Such a marriage cannot be undertaken in the Lord. And the man himself, having been polluted with his concubine before, now becomes an adulterer by marrying another; and she who is married to him gives consent to his adultery by this act of hers.

III. No man professing the Christian religion, much less a minister of the word, ought to take in marriage a harlot, a woman of ill fame, or one who comes of infamous parents, even though she is repentant. Lev 21:7: “A priest must not marry a woman who is a prostitute or profaned,” that is, one who has an evil name, or comes of disgraced parents. This law was in part ceremonial and Levitical, and so far abrogated. Yet since it has something moral in it, in that its reason (namely, because it is a reproach to a Christian, but especially to a minister) is still in force, it is to be held perpetual. For the profession of the Christian faith and the ministry of the church are chiefly to be respected, and whatever may harm their dignity and good name is to be avoided.

#emph[Objection 1.] The prophet #emph[Hosea] was commanded to take to himself a harlot: “Go, take a prostitute as your wife” (Hos 1:2). #emph[Answer.] #emph[Hosea] did not do it in deed, but in type and figure. For God commanded him in his prophecy to take on himself, as it were, the person of a fornicator, so that by this means he might the more effectually charge and convict the Israelites of their unclean and unchaste conduct.

#emph[Objection 2.] The Lord also forbids the priest to marry a widow (Lev 21:14). It may seem inexpedient, therefore, for a minister to do so. #emph[Answer.] The law about marrying a virgin and not a widow was not laid on all priests, but only on the chief priest (Lev 21:10). And he too might marry a widow, but only one who had been the widow of a priest, and no other (Ezek 44:22). Now this was commanded them for a special reason. For the high priest was a type of Christ, and Christ was in time to come to call his church, the elect, from among the Gentiles, and to marry them as a virgin betrothed to himself. Indeed, he was also to marry his church as a widow, but the widow of a priest only, because he was to gather to himself the remnant of the church of the Jews; yet not all of them, but only those who came of the holy fathers, and were Israelites not according to the flesh but according to the promise.

IV. It is not fitting for a man to be contracted to the sister of the woman who is already betrothed to him.

And so much for the first point required to make a contract, namely, the choice of a person fit for marriage.
